1. **A computer-implemented method for selecting reserved tone carrier indices in a multicarrier communication signal**, the method comprising:

   generating, by one or more processors, an initial set of candidate reserved tone carrier indices from a plurality of available carrier indices of the multicarrier communication signal;

   forming a kernel signal corresponding to the initial set of candidate reserved tone carrier indices, the kernel signal representing a time-domain response associated with placement of reserved tones at the candidate reserved tone carrier indices;

   determining, for each of a plurality of comparison arrangements of the candidate reserved tone carrier indices, a kernel signal comparison metric based on comparison of a kernel signal associated with the comparison arrangement to one or more reference averages;

   computing the one or more reference averages from magnitude, power, correlation, peak, sidelobe, or statistical values of kernel signals associated with at least a portion of the plurality of comparison arrangements;

   rearranging at least one index of the initial set of candidate reserved tone carrier indices to generate a rearranged set of reserved tone carrier indices;

   comparing a kernel signal corresponding to the rearranged set with the one or more reference averages to determine whether the rearranged set improves the kernel signal comparison metric;

   replacing, in response to the rearranged set improving the kernel signal comparison metric, at least one reserved tone carrier index of a current selected set with at least one rearranged index of the rearranged set;

   iteratively repeating the rearranging, comparing, and replacing until a stopping condition is satisfied; and

   outputting, after satisfaction of the stopping condition, a final selected set of reserved tone carrier indices for use in peak reduction, tone reservation, crest-factor reduction, or transmit-signal conditioning of the multicarrier communication signal.

2. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the kernel signal comparison metric comprises a deviation between a kernel signal magnitude profile and a reference average magnitude profile.

3. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the one or more reference averages comprise an average peak value, an average sidelobe value, an average time-domain magnitude value, an average correlation value, or a weighted combination thereof.

4. **The method of claim 1**, wherein rearranging at least one index comprises permuting, shifting, swapping, replacing, or randomly reordering one or more candidate reserved tone carrier indices within an allowed carrier-index range.

5. **The method of claim 1**, wherein replacing at least one reserved tone carrier index is performed only when the rearranged set produces a kernel signal having a reduced peak magnitude, reduced sidelobe energy, reduced correlation with data carriers, or reduced deviation from the one or more reference averages.

6. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the stopping condition comprises completion of a predetermined number of iterations, convergence of the kernel signal comparison metric, failure to improve the metric over a predetermined number of iterations, or achievement of a threshold metric value.

7. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising excluding unavailable carrier indices, data carrier indices, pilot carrier indices, guard-band indices, or restricted spectral-mask indices from selection as reserved tone carrier indices.

8. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the final selected set of reserved tone carrier indices is provided to a tone-reservation peak-reduction module configured to generate cancellation tones on the final selected set.

9. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium** storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform the method of any preceding claim.

10. **An apparatus for selecting reserved tone carrier indices**, comprising:

   memory configured to store available carrier-index information and kernel signal data; and

   one or more processors configured to generate candidate reserved tone carrier index sets, compute kernel signals for the candidate sets, determine kernel signal comparison metrics relative to reference averages, iteratively rearrange and replace indices based on improvement of the comparison metrics, and output a final selected set of reserved tone carrier indices.

A method is provided for determining reserved tones in a multicarrier communication system, the method comprising selecting one or more carrier indices for use as reserved tones based on comparison reference averages associated with candidate carrier indices and a kernel signal metric computed for the candidate carrier indices.

In one embodiment, a multicarrier signal comprises a plurality of carriers indexed over a predetermined carrier index range. A subset of the carriers is available for data modulation, and another subset is to be designated as reserved tones, for example for peak-to-average power ratio reduction, interference shaping, spectral shaping, calibration, synchronization, or other non-data-bearing functions. The reserved tones are selected so as to provide favorable distribution characteristics in a kernel signal associated with the selected tones.

The method includes obtaining, for each of a plurality of candidate carrier indices, a comparison reference average. The comparison reference average may be retrieved from a memory, table, register, look-up structure, or other storage element. Alternatively, the comparison reference average may be calculated during operation based on system parameters, including at least one of a number of carriers, a carrier spacing, a symbol length, an oversampling factor, an inverse transform size, a guard band configuration, a previously selected reserved-tone set, or a candidate-tone search range.

For each candidate carrier index, a kernel signal associated with selecting that carrier index is evaluated. The kernel signal may correspond to a time-domain contribution produced by assigning a predetermined value, weighting, or basis vector to the candidate carrier. In an orthogonal frequency division multiplexing system, the kernel signal may be derived from an inverse discrete Fourier transform component associated with the candidate carrier index. The kernel signal may also be a composite kernel signal corresponding to a current set of selected reserved tones combined with a candidate additional reserved tone.

A kernel signal metric is then determined for each candidate carrier index. The kernel signal metric may include, for example, a peak value, an average value, a peak-to-average ratio, a maximum absolute amplitude, an energy concentration value, a correlation value, a variance, a deviation from a desired kernel envelope, or a weighted combination of such quantities. In one implementation, the metric is determined by comparing an average characteristic of the kernel signal with the comparison reference average corresponding to the candidate carrier index.

The comparison reference average may represent an expected, desired, nominal, or previously determined average value of the kernel signal metric for the candidate carrier index. In some embodiments, the comparison reference averages are precomputed for a given multicarrier configuration and stored for later use. In other embodiments, the averages are calculated adaptively in response to changes in system bandwidth, number of active carriers, reserved-tone constraints, modulation format, channel condition, transmit power configuration, or regulatory spectral mask requirements.

The method further comprises selecting, from among the candidate carrier indices, at least one carrier index that minimizes the kernel signal metric. In one embodiment, each candidate carrier index is temporarily added to a set of already selected reserved-tone indices, a corresponding composite kernel signal is generated, and a metric of the composite kernel signal is computed relative to the stored or calculated comparison reference average. The candidate carrier index yielding the minimum metric is selected as a reserved tone. The selected carrier index is then added to the reserved-tone set.

The selection process may be repeated iteratively until a desired number of reserved tones has been selected. At each iteration, the comparison reference averages may remain fixed, may be updated from memory, or may be recalculated based on the current selected reserved-tone set. The resulting set of reserved-tone carrier indices is then provided to a transmitter, receiver, scheduler, tone reservation module, inverse transform processor, or peak-reduction processor.

In one exemplary implementation, the method comprises:

1. defining a candidate set of carrier indices in a multicarrier symbol;
2. obtaining comparison reference averages for the candidate carrier indices, the comparison reference averages being stored or calculated;
3. initializing a reserved-tone set;
4. for each candidate carrier index not already in the reserved-tone set, forming a kernel signal corresponding to inclusion of that candidate carrier index;
5. determining a kernel signal metric by comparing the kernel signal with the comparison reference average associated with the candidate carrier index;
6. identifying the candidate carrier index having the minimum kernel signal metric;
7. adding the identified candidate carrier index to the reserved-tone set; and
8. repeating the forming, determining, identifying, and adding steps until a predetermined number of reserved tones has been obtained.

The method may be embodied in a transmitter apparatus comprising processing circuitry configured to perform the foregoing operations. The processing circuitry may include one or more of a digital signal processor, application-specific integrated circuit, field-programmable gate array, controller, memory, inverse fast Fourier transform processor, or tone reservation controller. The memory may store the comparison reference averages, candidate carrier indices, selected reserved-tone indices, and kernel signal metric values.

The selected reserved tones may thereafter be used to generate a correction signal for reducing peaks in a time-domain multicarrier waveform. In such an embodiment, the reserved tones are not used to carry user data, but instead are modulated with correction symbols determined to reduce a peak amplitude, crest factor, or peak-to-average power ratio of the transmitted multicarrier signal.

By selecting carrier indices that minimize the kernel signal metric using stored or calculated comparison reference averages, the method enables efficient determination of reserved tones without exhaustively evaluating all possible reserved-tone combinations. The technique may thereby reduce computational complexity while improving or maintaining the effectiveness of tone reservation in a multicarrier communication system.
